{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_gaussian_column_split","record":{"id":"scalar.source_gaussian_column_split","kind":"claim","label":"Splitting one Gaussian column preserves the expected quadratic maximum","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r6-source-gaussian-column-split","locator":"GaussianColumn.lean:158–181; OAI.SKValueG.quadraticValue_column","statement":"For a finite candidate family w_i, real offsets d_i and a direction v that is zero or unit, quadraticValue(w,d) equals the Gaussian expectation of quadraticValue(columnResidual(v,w), d + the columnVector Gaussian linear process).","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar.source_gaussian_column_split_lean_source"],"boundary":"Source-inspected statement with quantified guards retained. Authored proof correspondence, not elaborated dependency extraction or a fresh formal acceptance."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_gaussian_column_split","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-column-split","anchor":"gaussian-column-split","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-column-split","anchor":"gaussian-column-split","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"gaussian-column-split\"></a>\n\n#### 10.2.1 Revealing one Gaussian column\n\nFor $w\\in\\mathbb R^n$, put $Q(w)=w\\otimes w/\\sqrt2$.\nAn independent standard Gaussian matrix $G$ gives a centered field\n$\\langle Q(w),G\\rangle$ with covariance\n$\\langle w,w'\\rangle^2/2$. Given a unit vector $v$, define\n$$\nR_vw=w-\\langle v,w\\rangle v,\\qquad\nC_v(w)=\\langle v,w\\rangle R_vw+\n       \\frac{\\langle v,w\\rangle^2}{\\sqrt2}v.\n$$\nWrite $\\alpha=\\langle v,w\\rangle$, $\\beta=\\langle v,w'\\rangle$\nand $r=\\langle R_vw,R_vw'\\rangle$. For an independent standard Gaussian\ncolumn $Z$ and matrix $G'$, the covariance of\n$\\langle C_v(w),Z\\rangle+\\langle Q(R_vw),G'\\rangle$ is\n$$\n\\alpha\\beta r+\\frac12\\alpha^2\\beta^2+\\frac12r^2\n=\\frac12(r+\\alpha\\beta)^2\n=\\frac12\\langle w,w'\\rangle^2.\n$$\nThus the new field has the old joint Gaussian law. The same statement\nholds for $v=0$: then $R_v$ is the identity and $C_v=0$.\nAllowing that case will avoid assuming empirical linear independence.\n\nFor a finite nonempty family of candidates $w_s$ and offsets $d_s$, define\n$$\n\\mathcal V(w,d)=\\mathbb E_G\\max_s\n  \\{\\langle Q(w_s),G\\rangle+d_s\\}.\n$$\nThe covariance identity and Fubini give the exact recursion\n$$\n\\mathcal V(w,d)=\\mathbb E_Z\\mathcal V\n \\bigl((R_vw_s)_s,(d_s+\\langle C_v(w_s),Z\\rangle)_s\\bigr).\n$$\nAlso $d_s\\le\\mathcal V(w,d)$ for each $s$, since the residual Gaussian\nterm has mean zero. These are the two facts needed for adaptive choices.\nThe source derives the equality by Gaussian comparison in both directions\nand proves the required integrability in\n[the one-column decomposition][scalar-column].\n","span":{"start_byte":183601,"end_byte":185182,"start_line":3916,"end_line":3957},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-column-split","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-column-split"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_gaussian_column_split&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_gaussian_column_split&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_gaussian_column_split&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_gaussian_column_split&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower%22%2C%22scalar.source_gaussian_column_split%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_finite_adaptive_lower","to":"sha256:9a43c